1. The M1 baseline (NRCan) only looks up fuel ratings, so the sitemap is built around what it lacks: Calculate Trip, My Garage and Saved Trips. The top tasks from the brief decided which pages exist, and the principles (Clarity, Consistency, Accessibility) shaped the layouts.

2. Exported views of the sitemap, journeys, and Home, Trip Results and Add Vehicle at all three sizes are in the report. The editable originals stay in Figma.

3. Navigation is a hamburger menu on mobile and full links on tablet and desktop. Trip Results adds a route map, round-trip checkbox and extra panels on desktop. Add Vehicle is one column on mobile and two on desktop. Model, Year and Trim stay locked until a make is chosen.

4. I did a scenario-based heuristic walkthrough with no participant. It led to five revisions: "+ Add a vehicle" in the dropdown, Account on Home, "Change vehicle" label, and Engine/trim on mobile. Limitations: no real user testing and data sources not final. M3: choose data sources, build the pages in code, test with real users.

